      </p>
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Welcome students to Unit 2: System Models of Distributed Systems.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 1: SYSTEM MODELS DEFINITION & REALITIES
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="distributed-systems-def"
    eyebrow="01 · FOUNDATIONS"
    title="What Is a Distributed System?"
    subtitle="Independent Machines Cooperating Through Network Communication"
    layout="split-sidebar"
    unit="BIT452CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        A distributed system is a collection of autonomous computers that work together to provide a unified service. Each machine has its own processor, memory, and operating system. The computers do not share physical memory, and no central quartz clock synchronizes their operations.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        To a user, the system appears as a single application. Behind the scenes, it coordinates across dozens or thousands of nodes. Common examples include cloud platforms, distributed databases, streaming networks, payment systems, and web search engines.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        Because components execute concurrently and communicate over non-instantaneous networks, reasoning about their behavior is difficult. System models provide formal abstractions to analyze and verify these systems systematically.
      </p>
    </div>

    <KeyTerms
      terms={[
        { term: 'Distributed System', definition: 'Independent computers communicating via a network to present a single coherent service to users.' },
        { term: 'Local State', definition: 'The private memory, registers, and storage contents maintained exclusively on an individual node.' },
        { term: 'Shared-Nothing Memory', definition: 'An architecture where computing nodes share neither physical RAM nor disk storage directly.' },
      ]}
    />

    <SpeakerNotes>
      Emphasize the core definition: independent machines, private memory, communication via messages. Contrast this directly with multi-threaded programs on a single machine.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 2: INTRODUCTION TO SYSTEM MODELS
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="intro-system-models"
    eyebrow="01 · FOUNDATIONS"
    title="Introduction to System Models: Why We Use Abstraction"
    layout="card-grid"
    unit="BIT452CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-5 my-auto">
      <Card title="What a Model Is" badge="Abstract View" glow="indigo">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Capturing essential characteristics.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          A system model is an abstract representation of a distributed system. It highlights important properties while deliberately ignoring low-level implementation details that are not relevant to the analysis.
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="What a Model Defines" badge="Explicit Rules" glow="cyan">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Establishing clear assumptions.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          A model establishes explicit assumptions about participating components, communication channels, execution speeds, message delays, and possible failure modes.
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="Why We Need It" badge="Design & Proof" glow="rose">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Tractable problem solving.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          The goal is not to replicate every physical wire and chip. The goal is to create a precise baseline for proving whether an algorithm will work correctly under real-world conditions.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Explain that all scientific engineering uses models. In distributed systems, models prevent engineers from making incorrect assumptions about network speeds or node reliability.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 3: WHY SYSTEM MODELS ARE NECESSARY
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="why-models-necessary"
    eyebrow="01 · FOUNDATIONS"
    title="Why System Models Are Necessary: Three Core Difficulties"
    layout="split-sidebar"
    unit="BIT452CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        Distributed applications contain many concurrent software components running on separate machines. Each machine has its own processor, memory, storage, operating system, and local clock.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        First, communication takes time. A sender cannot know the exact microsecond a receiver gets a message. Second, different processes execute at varying speeds. Third, components fail independently: a server can crash or a switch can drop packets while the rest of the cluster stays healthy.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        These factors make distributed systems difficult to understand. System models reduce complexity by defining consistent assumptions for design and analysis.
      </p>
    </div>

    <KeyTerms
      terms={[
        { term: 'Non-Instantaneous Transit', definition: 'The physical reality that network packet propagation consumes variable, non-zero time.' },
        { term: 'Partial Failure', definition: 'A condition where a subset of distributed components fails while other components continue running.' },
        { term: 'Execution Skew', definition: 'Discrepancies in computation speeds caused by differing CPU loads and hardware capabilities.' },
      ]}
    />

    <SpeakerNotes>
      Clarify why distributed systems are fundamentally harder than centralized ones: partial failure and lack of instantaneous observation.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 4: ABSTRACTION IN DISTRIBUTED SYSTEMS
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="abstraction-principles"
    eyebrow="01 · FOUNDATIONS"
    title="Abstraction in Distributed Systems: Isolating Relevant Properties"
    layout="split-sidebar"
    unit="BIT452CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        Abstraction represents a complex system by focusing only on the characteristics required for a specific purpose, filtering out irrelevant physical details.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        Consider a cluster of hundreds of servers. When analyzing communication protocols, we do not need to know the specific processor model in every server. We need to know whether messages can be delayed or lost, and how long transmission can take.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        Similarly, when studying fault tolerance, the internal code structure of a server is less important than its failure behavior: whether it halts completely upon crashing, whether it can recover from disk, and whether peers can detect the failure.
      </p>
    </div>

    <KeyTerms
      terms={[
        { term: 'Abstraction Boundary', definition: 'The division separating essential system behaviors from irrelevant internal implementation details.' },
        { term: 'Safety Property', definition: 'A formal algorithmic guarantee that nothing bad happens during execution.' },
        { term: 'Liveness Property', definition: 'A formal algorithmic guarantee that the system eventually makes forward progress.' },
      ]}
    />

    <SpeakerNotes>
      Point out that abstraction is not just an academic tool; it is the fundamental reason cloud platforms like AWS or Azure can scale globally.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 5: CHARACTERISTICS OF A DISTRIBUTED SYSTEM
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="system-characteristics"
    eyebrow="01 · FOUNDATIONS"
    title="Four Fundamental Characteristics of Distributed Systems"
    layout="card-grid"
    unit="BIT452CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-auto">
      <Card title="1. Concurrent Computation" badge="Parallelism" glow="indigo">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Independent processors executing in parallel.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Multiple computers participate in the computation concurrently. Each maintains its own local memory, instruction pipeline, and private state.
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="2. Network Message Exchange" badge="Communication" glow="cyan">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Communication takes non-zero time.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Communication occurs through messages across networks. Network latency is variable and can be affected by congestion, routing changes, or hardware faults.
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="3. No Global Clock" badge="Time & Ordering" glow="amber">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Independent physical quartz crystals.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          There is no single shared clock that all processes observe with perfect accuracy, making exact event ordering across different machines challenging.
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="4. Independent Failures" badge="Fault Semantics" glow="rose">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Isolated node and link breakdowns.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          A computer, process, network link, or storage device can fail while surrounding components continue functioning, requiring explicit fault handling.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Walk through the four pillars. Highlight that the absence of a global clock directly forces distributed algorithms to use logical timestamps.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 6: MAIN PERSPECTIVES OF SYSTEM MODELING
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="modeling-perspectives"
    eyebrow="01 · FOUNDATIONS"
    title="Main Perspectives of System Modeling"
    subtitle="Complementary Views for Complete System Understanding"
    layout="card-grid"
    unit="BIT452CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-auto">
      <Card title="Architectural Model" badge="Structure & Placement" glow="indigo">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">How components are organized.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Describes how the major components of the distributed system are arranged, where they execute, and how responsibilities are divided among them.
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="Interaction Model" badge="Communication & Time" glow="cyan">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">How processes communicate and time behaves.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Describes how processes exchange messages, how events occur in sequence, and what timing bounds apply to computation speeds and transit delays.
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="Failure Model" badge="Fault Classification" glow="rose">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">How components malfunction.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Defines the specific ways computing processes and network links can fail, and how those failures affect system operations.
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="Security Model" badge="Adversarial Vectors" glow="amber">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">How systems protect against threats.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Describes the capabilities of potential attackers, vulnerabilities like eavesdropping or tampering, and required security protections.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Emphasize that these models are complementary: a complete system specification requires answering questions across architecture, interaction, and failure modes.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 7: ARCHITECTURAL MODEL OVERVIEW
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="architectural-model-overview"
    eyebrow="02 · ARCHITECTURAL MODELS"
    title="Architectural Model: Structural Organization and Role Assignment"
    layout="split-sidebar"
    unit="BIT452CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        An architectural model describes the organization of a distributed system at a high level. It identifies the major components of the system, the services provided by those components, and the relationships between them.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        For example, an online banking application contains user devices, web servers, application servers, authentication services, database servers, caching systems, and external payment gateways.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        The architectural model does not describe the internal code implementation of each component. Instead, it explains how these components are arranged and how they cooperate to deliver the overall service.
      </p>
    </div>

    <KeyTerms
      terms={[
        { term: 'Architectural Model', definition: 'The structural specification of components, roles, interfaces, and inter-service relationships.' },
        { term: 'Service Boundary', definition: 'The explicit interface contract defining the inputs, outputs, and protocols of an autonomous service.' },
        { term: 'Deployment Topology', definition: 'The physical or virtual mapping of software components across network nodes and data centers.' },
      ]}
    />

    <SpeakerNotes>
      Explain how the architectural model serves as the master blueprint for system builders, dictating where data lives and how requests traverse the network.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 8: COMPONENTS OF A DISTRIBUTED ARCHITECTURE
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="distributed-components"
    eyebrow="02 · ARCHITECTURAL MODELS"
    title="Core Components of a Distributed Architecture"
    layout="card-grid"
    unit="BIT452CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-4 my-auto">
      <Card title="Processes" badge="Execution" glow="indigo">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Programs in execution.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Programs executing on local or remote nodes that perform computation and maintain local state. Multiple processes execute on the same or different computers.
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="Services" badge="Interfaces" glow="cyan">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Functionality for other components.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Capabilities provided by one component for use by another, such as database query execution or authentication validation.
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="Channels" badge="Transport" glow="rose">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Mechanisms for message exchange.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Network paths and socket protocols through which distributed processes exchange formatted data packets.
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="Data Resources" badge="Persistence" glow="amber">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Shared information stores.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Databases, files, objects, cached records, and shared data resources accessed concurrently by multiple application components.
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="Middleware" badge="Coordination" glow="indigo">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Distribution abstraction layer.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Higher-level software providing naming, RPC, RMI, authentication, and transaction services to simplify distributed coordination.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Review these 5 core structural primitives. Every distributed system, from a small classroom cluster to AWS, is constructed from these basic building blocks.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 9: PROCESSES IN A DISTRIBUTED SYSTEM
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="process-abstraction"
    eyebrow="02 · ARCHITECTURAL MODELS"
    title="Processes in Distributed Systems: Independent Execution and State"
    layout="split-sidebar"
    unit="BIT452CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        A process is an executing instance of a program. In a centralized system, several processes can communicate using mechanisms provided by the operating system, including shared memory.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        In a distributed system, processes located on different computers cannot directly access each other's local memory. Each process maintains its own private local state.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        If one process requires information held by another, it must obtain that information through explicit network communication. For example, an application server requests customer records from a database process, which queries disk and replies.
      </p>
    </div>

    <KeyTerms
      terms={[
        { term: 'Process Isolation', definition: 'The physical and kernel memory boundaries that prevent external processes from directly accessing local state.' },
        { term: 'Address Space', definition: 'The range of virtual memory addresses allocated exclusively to a specific running process.' },
        { term: 'State Encapsulation', definition: 'The principle that internal state can only be inspected or modified through explicit message protocols.' },
      ]}
    />

    <SpeakerNotes>
      Reinforce that in distributed systems, pointer dereferencing across machines is impossible. Everything must go through serialized message buffers.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 10: COMMUNICATION IN DISTRIBUTED SYSTEMS
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="communication-mechanisms"
    eyebrow="02 · ARCHITECTURAL MODELS"
    title="Communication in Distributed Systems: Message Passing Mechanics"
    layout="default"
    unit="BIT452CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 my-auto items-center">
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-base font-extrabold text-slate-950 m-0">The Request-Response Timeline</h3>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Communication between distributed processes occurs through message passing. A process generates a message containing a request and sends it across the network. The receiver gets the message, executes the operation, and replies.
        </p>
        <div class="p-3 rounded-lg bg-slate-100 border-2 border-slate-300 space-y-1.5 text-xs text-slate-800 font-mono">
          <div>1. Process A formats and dispatches Request</div>
          <div>2. Message traverses routers with variable delay</div>
          <div>3. Process B receives, parses, and processes</div>
          <div>4. Process B formats Response and transmits back</div>
          <div>5. Process A receives Response and resumes work</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border-2 border-slate-800 text-slate-100 font-mono text-xs shadow-md">
        <div class="text-rose-400 font-bold mb-2">// Network Message Exchange Trace</div>
        <div class="text-slate-300">Client Node: 10.0.1.4  -->  Server Node: 10.0.2.18</div>
        <div class="text-slate-500">-----------------------------------------------</div>
        <div class="text-emerald-400">[00.000 ms] Client dispatches POST /api/transfer</div>
        <div class="text-amber-400">[00.018 ms] Network propagation transit (WAN)</div>
        <div class="text-sky-400">[00.022 ms] Server unmarshalls request payload</div>
        <div class="text-sky-400">[00.035 ms] Server commits transaction to DB</div>
        <div class="text-amber-400">[00.040 ms] Network response transit (WAN)</div>
        <div class="text-emerald-400">[00.058 ms] Client receives HTTP 200 OK</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Walk students through the network round-trip time (RTT). Contrast the 0.001 ms local memory lookup with the 50 ms transatlantic network trip.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 11: MIDDLEWARE
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="middleware-role"
    eyebrow="02 · ARCHITECTURAL MODELS"
    title="Middleware: Abstraction Layer for Distributed Applications"
    layout="split-sidebar"
    unit="BIT452CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        Middleware is software that provides a common abstraction for communication and coordination between distributed applications. It sits between application programs and lower-level operating-system and network services.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        Middleware provides facilities such as Remote Procedure Calls (RPC), Remote Method Invocation (RMI), message passing, naming, service discovery, authentication, serialization, and distributed transactions.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        The primary purpose of middleware is to hide distribution complexities. For example, Java RMI allows an application to invoke methods on objects located in another Java Virtual Machine as if they were local references.
      </p>
    </div>

    <KeyTerms
      terms={[
        { term: 'Middleware', definition: 'Software providing common abstractions for communication, discovery, and coordination across distributed nodes.' },
        { term: 'Remote Method Invocation', definition: 'A mechanism allowing a process to execute an object method on a remote virtual machine transparently.' },
        { term: 'Marshalling', definition: 'The process of packing structured memory objects into serialized byte streams suitable for network transmission.' },
      ]}
    />

    <SpeakerNotes>
      Explain how middleware prevents programmers from writing raw socket code, socket buffers, and endianness conversions for every single service.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 12: SOFTWARE LAYERS
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="software-layers"
    eyebrow="02 · ARCHITECTURAL MODELS"
    title="Software Layering in Distributed Systems"
    layout="default"
    unit="BIT452CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 my-auto items-center">
      <div class="space-y-2">
        <div class="p-3 rounded-lg bg-rose-50 border-2 border-rose-300 text-xs font-bold text-slate-900 flex justify-between items-center">
          <span>Applications</span>
          <span class="text-rose-800 font-mono">User & business functionality</span>
        </div>
        <div class="p-3 rounded-lg bg-indigo-50 border-2 border-indigo-300 text-xs font-bold text-slate-900 flex justify-between items-center">
          <span>Middleware</span>
          <span class="text-indigo-800 font-mono">RPC, RMI, naming, coordination</span>
        </div>
        <div class="p-3 rounded-lg bg-teal-50 border-2 border-teal-300 text-xs font-bold text-slate-900 flex justify-between items-center">
          <span>Operating System</span>
          <span class="text-teal-800 font-mono">Local processor, memory, storage</span>
        </div>
        <div class="p-3 rounded-lg bg-amber-50 border-2 border-amber-300 text-xs font-bold text-slate-900 flex justify-between items-center">
          <span>Network / Communication</span>
          <span class="text-amber-800 font-mono">Packet routing, sockets, transport</span>
        </div>
        <div class="p-3 rounded-lg bg-slate-100 border-2 border-slate-300 text-xs font-bold text-slate-900 flex justify-between items-center">
          <span>Hardware</span>
          <span class="text-slate-700 font-mono">CPUs, memory, network interfaces, disks</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-base font-extrabold text-slate-950 m-0">Layer Encapsulation Benefits</h3>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Layering reduces complexity by allowing each layer to provide well-defined services to the layer above it.
        </p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Application developers focus on business logic using middleware abstractions, while the network and operating system manage physical transmission and local hardware.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Walk students through each layer from bottom (Hardware) to top (Applications). Show how each layer solves a specific level of complexity.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 13: APPLICATION LAYER
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="application-layer"
    eyebrow="02 · ARCHITECTURAL MODELS"
    title="Application Layer: Multi-Component Service Delivery"
    layout="split-sidebar"
    unit="BIT452CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        The application layer contains programs that implement the functionality required by users or other applications. Examples include online banking, e-commerce platforms, streaming services, cloud storage, and enterprise resource planning systems.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        An application that appears to be a single program to a user may consist of many distributed components running across multiple servers.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        For example, when a user logs into an online banking system, the request may pass through a frontend server, an authentication service, an application server, a database, and a security service before the final response is returned.
      </p>
    </div>

    <KeyTerms
      terms={[
        { term: 'Application Layer', definition: 'The highest software layer delivering domain logic and user interactions in a distributed system.' },
        { term: 'Service Orchestration', definition: 'The automated coordination and sequence of multiple independent services to fulfill a single transaction.' },
        { term: 'Unified Interface', definition: 'A client presentation that conceals the underlying multi-service topology from the user.' },
      ]}
    />

    <SpeakerNotes>
      Explain how a single click on Amazon or Netflix triggers dozens of microservices across multiple datacenters.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 14: OPERATING SYSTEM LAYER
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="operating-system-layer"
    eyebrow="02 · ARCHITECTURAL MODELS"
    title="Operating System Layer: Local Resource Management and Independence"
    layout="split-sidebar"
    unit="BIT452CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        Every computer participating in a distributed system has its own operating system. The operating system manages local resources, including the processor, memory, storage devices, network interfaces, and running processes.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        The operating system provides system calls and services that applications use to perform local operations, allocate memory, and manage sockets.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        Although the computers cooperate as one distributed system, their operating systems remain independent. A process running on one computer cannot directly manipulate the memory or processor of another computer. Communication requires network mechanisms.
      </p>
    </div>

    <KeyTerms
      terms={[
        { term: 'OS Autonomy', definition: 'The independence of each host kernel in managing its own local processor scheduling and memory allocation.' },
        { term: 'Kernel Protection Boundary', definition: 'The hardware-enforced separation preventing remote processes from manipulating local memory spaces.' },
        { term: 'Network Socket API', definition: 'The OS abstraction providing endpoints for sending and receiving byte streams over physical networks.' },
      ]}
    />

    <SpeakerNotes>
      Emphasize that the OS does not know it is in a distributed cluster; it simply manages its own hardware and network sockets.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 15: SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="system-architecture-overview"
    eyebrow="02 · ARCHITECTURAL MODELS"
