  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 16: CLIENT-SERVER ARCHITECTURE
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="client-server-arch"
    eyebrow="02 · ARCHITECTURAL MODELS"
    title="Client-Server Architecture: Core Roles"
    layout="default"
    unit="BIT452CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 my-auto items-center">
      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-slate-100 border-2 border-slate-300 font-mono text-xs space-y-2">
        <div class="text-slate-950 font-bold text-sm mb-3">Client-Server Structural Topology</div>
        <div class="p-2 rounded bg-indigo-50 border border-indigo-200 text-indigo-900">Client Node 1 ──┐</div>
        <div class="p-2 rounded bg-indigo-50 border border-indigo-200 text-indigo-900">Client Node 2 ──┼──> [ Centralized Server Engine ]</div>
        <div class="p-2 rounded bg-indigo-50 border border-indigo-200 text-indigo-900">Client Node 3 ──┘       (Processes & Responds)</div>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-base font-extrabold text-slate-950 m-0">Division of Responsibilities</h3>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          The client is a process that requests a service. The server is a process that provides that service. The client sends a request to the server, and the server processes it and returns a response.
        </p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          A web browser communicating with a web server is a common example. The browser acts as the client, while the web server provides web resources or application services.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Highlight the role asymmetry: clients initiate; servers listen. Servers must be provisioned with high uptime and public network availability.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 17: REQUEST AND RESPONSE
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="request-response-pattern"
    eyebrow="02 · ARCHITECTURAL MODELS"
    title="Request-Response Interaction Pattern"
    layout="split-sidebar"
    unit="BIT452CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        The request-response pattern is one of the most common forms of interaction in client-server systems.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        The client initiates communication by sending a request that identifies the required operation and provides necessary parameters. The server receives the request, performs the processing, accesses resources if necessary, and generates a response.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        For example, a web browser requests a webpage over HTTP. The server processes the request, retrieves content from the file system or database, and sends an HTTP response back to the browser.
      </p>
    </div>

    <KeyTerms
      terms={[
        { term: 'Request-Response', definition: 'A conversational protocol where a client request triggers explicit server computation and reply.' },
        { term: 'Idempotency', definition: 'A property where repeating an identical request multiple times produces the exact same system state.' },
        { term: 'Synchronous Wait', definition: 'A client execution state that blocks thread progress until the server response is received.' },
      ]}
    />

    <SpeakerNotes>
      Explain how request-response mirrors local function invocation: caller passes arguments, callee computes and returns a return value.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 18: ADVANTAGES AND LIMITATIONS OF CLIENT-SERVER
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="client-server-tradeoffs"
    eyebrow="02 · ARCHITECTURAL MODELS"
    title="Advantages and Limitations of Client-Server Architecture"
    layout="card-grid"
    unit="BIT452CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-5 my-auto">
      <Card title="Advantages" badge="Centralized Control" glow="indigo">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Clear role separation.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Centralizing critical services simplifies administration, security management, and resource allocation. Shared resources are maintained in a single controlled environment.
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="Limitations" badge="Bottlenecks" glow="rose">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Single point of failure.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          A heavily used server can become a performance bottleneck. If a critical server fails, dependent clients lose access to the service.
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="Mitigations" badge="Scaling Techniques" glow="cyan">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Handling high demand.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          These bottlenecks are addressed through server replication, load balancing, proxy caching, data partitioning, and automated failover mechanisms.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Discuss the classic engineering dilemma: centralized architectures are easy to manage, but become single points of failure without horizontal replication.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 19: TWO-TIER ARCHITECTURE
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="two-tier-architecture"
    eyebrow="02 · ARCHITECTURAL MODELS"
    title="Two-Tier Architecture: Client and Database Server"
    layout="split-sidebar"
    unit="BIT452CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        Two-tier architecture divides a distributed application into two major tiers: a client tier and a server tier.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        The client tier contains the user interface and application logic. The server tier provides data management services. A traditional desktop application communicating directly with a database server is a common example.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        Two-tier architecture is straightforward for small systems. However, as client numbers grow, direct client-to-database connections create scalability, security, and maintenance difficulties.
      </p>
    </div>

    <KeyTerms
      terms={[
        { term: 'Two-Tier Architecture', definition: 'A client-server topology where client software connects directly to the database persistence layer.' },
        { term: 'Connection Exhaustion', definition: 'Depletion of database server connection pools caused by each client holding a persistent socket.' },
        { term: 'Schema Coupling', definition: 'Direct client dependency on database table structures, making schema changes breaking.' },
      ]}
    />

    <SpeakerNotes>
      Show why two-tier systems were popular in the 1990s desktop era, and why the internet forced everyone to adopt three-tier architectures.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

