       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="peer-to-peer-arch"
    eyebrow="02 · ARCHITECTURAL MODELS"
    title="Peer-to-Peer Architecture: Symmetric Nodes"
    layout="split-sidebar"
    unit="BIT452CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        In a peer-to-peer architecture, participating nodes can act both as service providers and service consumers. There is no requirement for a permanent central server.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        A peer may request information from another peer while simultaneously providing information to other peers in the network. Processing, storage, and communication responsibilities are distributed among participating nodes.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        However, decentralization introduces additional engineering challenges involving node discovery, coordination, security, consistency, and handling dynamic node departures.
      </p>
    </div>

    <KeyTerms
      terms={[
        { term: 'Peer-to-Peer (P2P)', definition: 'A decentralized network architecture where participating nodes share symmetric roles and pool resources.' },
        { term: 'Node Churn', definition: 'The rapid, unpredictable joining and leaving of participating nodes in a peer-to-peer network.' },
        { term: 'Overlay Network', definition: 'A virtual computer network built on top of physical IP networks to route messages between peers.' },
      ]}
    />

    <SpeakerNotes>
      Contrast BitTorrent and Bitcoin with YouTube and AWS. Highlight the trade-off: high resilience and resource pooling vs complex coordination.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

