  <Slide
    id="client-server-variations"
    eyebrow="02 · ARCHITECTURAL MODELS"
    title="Variations of the Client-Server Model"
    layout="card-grid"
    unit="BIT452CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-auto">
      <Card title="Proxy Servers" badge="Intermediaries" glow="indigo">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Intermediary request routing.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Clients communicate through a proxy that forwards requests, applies security filters, translates protocols, or serves cached results.
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="Caching Layers" badge="Performance" glow="cyan">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Storing frequently requested data.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Caches store previously retrieved resources close to clients, reducing repeated network round-trips to origin servers.
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="Server Replication" badge="Availability" glow="rose">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Multiple identical server instances.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Replicated servers process requests in parallel behind load balancers, ensuring service continues if one instance fails.
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="Thin vs Fat Clients" badge="Workload Division" glow="amber">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Computation placement choices.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Balancing application execution between lightweight browser frontends and compute-intensive local client software.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
