  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 25: THIN CLIENT
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="thin-client-arch"
    eyebrow="02 · ARCHITECTURAL MODELS"
    title="Thin Client: Server-Centric Processing"
    layout="split-sidebar"
    unit="BIT452CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        A thin client is a client in which relatively little application processing is performed locally. Most of the computation and data processing are provided by remote servers.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        Web applications accessed through a browser are a common example. The browser handles presentation and basic user input, while business logic and database queries execute on remote servers.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        Thin clients simplify centralized management because application software is maintained primarily on servers. However, they depend heavily on continuous network connectivity and server availability.
      </p>
    </div>

    <KeyTerms
      terms={[
        { term: 'Thin Client', definition: 'A client architecture that relies on remote servers for most computational processing.' },
        { term: 'Centralized Maintenance', definition: 'Updating application code on servers without requiring user-side software installations.' },
        { term: 'Network Dependency', definition: 'The requirement for an active network connection for the client to function.' },
      ]}
    />

    <SpeakerNotes>
      Discuss Google Docs vs local Microsoft Word as the classic thin client vs fat client comparison.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 26: FAT CLIENT
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="fat-client-arch"
    eyebrow="02 · ARCHITECTURAL MODELS"
    title="Fat Client: Local Processing Power"
    layout="split-sidebar"
    unit="BIT452CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        A fat client, also called a thick client, performs a substantial amount of computational processing and business logic locally on the client machine.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        Desktop applications that perform complex calculations or graphics rendering locally while communicating with a central server for shared records are common examples.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        Fat clients reduce server processing demands and can operate during temporary network disconnections. However, distributing software updates, configuration changes, and security patches across many client machines is more difficult.
      </p>
    </div>

    <KeyTerms
      terms={[
        { term: 'Fat Client (Thick Client)', definition: 'A client that executes significant business logic and processing locally before contacting servers.' },
        { term: 'Offline Operation', definition: 'The ability of a client to perform tasks locally when disconnected from the network.' },
        { term: 'Software Distribution', definition: 'The process of deploying software updates and patches to individual user machines.' },
      ]}
    />

    <SpeakerNotes>
      Highlight the trade-off: rich local performance and offline capability vs administrative maintenance headaches.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 27: PROXY SERVERS
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="proxy-servers-caching"
    eyebrow="02 · ARCHITECTURAL MODELS"
    title="Proxy Servers: Intermediary Communication and Caching"
    layout="split-sidebar"
    unit="BIT452CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        A proxy server is an intermediary between a client and another server. Instead of communicating directly with the destination server, the client sends its request to the proxy.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        The proxy processes or forwards the request and returns the result to the client. Proxies provide caching, access control, security filtering, traffic management, and protocol translation.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        Caching is particularly useful when multiple clients request identical resources. The proxy stores previously retrieved resources and serves subsequent requests from cache, reducing network traffic and improving response times.
      </p>
    </div>

    <KeyTerms
      terms={[
        { term: 'Proxy Server', definition: 'An intermediary host that intercepts and handles requests between clients and destination servers.' },
        { term: 'Cache Storage', definition: 'Temporary high-speed local storage holding frequently accessed resources.' },
        { term: 'Traffic Management', definition: 'Filtering, routing, and optimizing data packets moving through a network boundary.' },
      ]}
    />

    <SpeakerNotes>
      Make sure students clearly understand the difference between a forward proxy (protects clients) and a reverse proxy (protects servers).
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 28: REPLICATED SERVERS
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="server-replication"
    eyebrow="02 · ARCHITECTURAL MODELS"
    title="Replicated Servers: Capacity and High Availability"
    layout="split-sidebar"
    unit="BIT452CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        Replication involves maintaining multiple copies or instances of a service or data resource across separate physical computers.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        Replication increases system capacity because multiple servers can process requests concurrently. It also improves availability: if one server fails, another instance can continue providing the service without interrupting users.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        However, replication introduces the challenge of maintaining consistency between copies, especially when replicated resources can be modified concurrently by multiple clients.
      </p>
    </div>

    <KeyTerms
      terms={[
        { term: 'Server Replication', definition: 'Maintaining multiple identical copies of a service or database across different machines.' },
        { term: 'High Availability', definition: 'A system characteristic ensuring services remain accessible even during hardware failures.' },
        { term: 'Consistency Challenge', definition: 'The difficulty of ensuring all replica copies reflect identical data after updates.' },
      ]}
    />

    <SpeakerNotes>
      Explain how replication solves availability, but introduces the hardest problem in distributed systems: keeping replicas synchronized.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 29: LOAD BALANCING
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="load-balancing-strategies"
    eyebrow="02 · ARCHITECTURAL MODELS"
    title="Load Balancing: Distributing Incoming Requests"
    layout="card-grid"
    unit="BIT452CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-auto">
      <Card title="Round-Robin" badge="Sequential Routing" glow="indigo">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Equal cyclical request allocation.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Distributes incoming client requests sequentially among server instances in the pool. Weighted variants allocate more traffic to higher-capacity servers.
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="Least Connections" badge="Workload Based" glow="cyan">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Dynamic active connection routing.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Directs new client connections to the server currently handling the smallest number of active requests, preventing slow queries from overloading a node.
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="Resource Utilization" badge="Telemetry Based" glow="rose">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Routing based on real-time metrics.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Monitors real-time server metrics like CPU utilization, memory pressure, and response time to route traffic away from heavily stressed nodes.
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="IP Hash / Affinity" badge="Session Sticky" glow="amber">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Preserving client session state.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Hashes client IP addresses or session identifiers to ensure returning requests from a specific user reach the exact same server instance.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Compare these 4 algorithms. Point out that modern cloud load balancers combine health checks with weighted least-connections.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 30: MOBILE CODE
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="mobile-code"
    eyebrow="02 · ARCHITECTURAL MODELS"
    title="Mobile Code: Moving Computation to Data"
    layout="split-sidebar"
    unit="BIT452CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        Mobile code refers to program code that can be transferred from one computer to another and executed in the receiving environment.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        Traditional distributed computing moves data across the network to a program for processing. With mobile code, the program can be moved closer to where the data is stored.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        For example, if a large dataset is stored on a remote server, transferring a small processing script to that system requires less network bandwidth than transferring the entire dataset across the wire. However, running external code requires strict security sandboxing.
      </p>
    </div>

    <KeyTerms
      terms={[
        { term: 'Mobile Code', definition: 'Program code transferred across a network to execute inside a remote runtime environment.' },
        { term: 'Compute-to-Data', definition: 'The strategy of moving code closer to stored data to conserve network bandwidth.' },
        { term: 'Sandboxing', definition: 'An isolated execution environment that restricts the permissions and resources of untrusted code.' },
      ]}
    />

    <SpeakerNotes>
      Point out that JavaScript in web browsers is the most common real-world example of mobile code: the server ships code that runs in the client's browser.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 31: MOBILE AGENTS
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="mobile-agents"
    eyebrow="02 · ARCHITECTURAL MODELS"
    title="Mobile Agents: Autonomous Multi-Host Execution"
    layout="split-sidebar"
    unit="BIT452CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        A mobile agent is a program that can move between computers or execution environments while continuing to perform a task.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        An agent executes part of its task on Host A, migrates its code and execution state across the network to Host B, continues processing there, and then moves to Host C.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        Mobile agents differ from ordinary mobile code because they are designed as autonomous programs capable of making decisions and carrying out multi-step activities across hosts. However, they introduce significant security, compatibility, and fault-tolerance challenges.
      </p>
    </div>

    <KeyTerms
      terms={[
        { term: 'Mobile Agent', definition: 'An autonomous program capable of migrating its code, state, and execution stack across multiple hosts.' },
        { term: 'State Migration', definition: 'The transfer and restoration of an active program execution state on a new node.' },
        { term: 'Host Itinerary', definition: 'The programmed sequence of host destinations an autonomous agent visits to complete its task.' },
      ]}
    />

    <SpeakerNotes>
      Explain why mobile agents were heavily researched in academic literature, but struggled in industry due to security and runtime heterogeneity.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 32: FUNDAMENTAL MODELS
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="fundamental-models-overview"
    eyebrow="03 · FUNDAMENTAL MODELS"
    title="Fundamental Models: Assumptions About System Behavior"
    layout="split-sidebar"
    unit="BIT452CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        Architectural models explain the structure and organization of a distributed system. Fundamental models describe assumptions about the behavior of that system.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        Two essential fundamental models are the interaction model and the failure model. The interaction model considers how processes execute and communicate, including timing and event ordering. The failure model considers how components fail and what incorrect behavior can result.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        These models are essential when designing distributed algorithms because an algorithm can only be considered correct relative to the assumptions made about its execution environment.
      </p>
    </div>

    <KeyTerms
      terms={[
        { term: 'Fundamental Model', definition: 'A formal specification of behavioral assumptions regarding timing, communication, and failures.' },
        { term: 'Algorithm Correctness', definition: 'Proof that an algorithm achieves safety and liveness under specified operational models.' },
        { term: 'Environmental Assumptions', definition: 'The explicit operational boundaries within which a distributed protocol functions.' },
      ]}
    />

    <SpeakerNotes>
      Emphasize the shift from structure (architecture) to behavioral rules (fundamental models).
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 33: INTERACTION MODEL
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="interaction-model-foundations"
    eyebrow="03 · FUNDAMENTAL MODELS"
    title="Interaction Model: Communication and Timing Assumptions"
    layout="card-grid"
    unit="BIT452CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-5 my-auto">
      <Card title="Concurrent Execution" badge="Processes" glow="indigo">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Overlapping instruction cycles.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Processes execute concurrently across separate machines. No single execution sequence can be directly observed by all participating processes.
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="Transmission Delays" badge="Network Latency" glow="cyan">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Variable message transit times.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Communication takes place through messages, and transmission incurs variable delays due to routing, buffering, and physical distance.
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="Event Ordering" badge="Clocks & Time" glow="rose">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Determining temporal relationships.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Because local clocks drift independently, determining the exact order of events occurring on different computers requires explicit coordination.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Review the 3 realities of the interaction model. Ask students: how do you know if Event A happened before Event B on two different machines?
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 34: SYNCHRONOUS DISTRIBUTED SYSTEMS
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="synchronous-systems"
    eyebrow="03 · FUNDAMENTAL MODELS"
    title="Synchronous Distributed Systems: Known Timing Bounds"
    layout="split-sidebar"
    unit="BIT452CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        A synchronous distributed system is one in which known upper bounds can be placed on important aspects of computation and communication.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        Specifically, the model assumes that a process takes no more than a specified maximum time to execute an operation, that a message takes no more than a specified time to travel between nodes, and that local clocks drift by at most a known rate.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        These assumptions simplify algorithm design because the absence of a response within a known timeout provides definitive information that a node has failed. However, real-world wide-area networks rarely guarantee strict synchronous bounds.
      </p>
    </div>

    <KeyTerms
      terms={[
        { term: 'Synchronous System', definition: 'A distributed system where known upper bounds exist for computation time, transmission delay, and clock drift.' },
        { term: 'Deterministic Timeout', definition: 'A time threshold beyond which the absence of a response confirms a failure has occurred.' },
        { term: 'Bounded Drift', definition: 'A guarantee that local physical clocks diverge from true time by no more than a known constant rate.' },
      ]}
    />

    <SpeakerNotes>
      Explain that synchronous systems are a theoretical ideal or confined to specialized hard real-time systems (automotive fly-by-wire, industrial control).
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 35: ASYNCHRONOUS DISTRIBUTED SYSTEMS
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="asynchronous-systems"
    eyebrow="03 · FUNDAMENTAL MODELS"
    title="Asynchronous Distributed Systems: Unbounded Timing"
    layout="split-sidebar"
    unit="BIT452CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        An asynchronous distributed system does not provide known fixed upper bounds on process execution time or message transmission latency.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        A process may execute slowly, or a message may be delayed in transit for an unpredictable duration without violating the model.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        This creates a fundamental difficulty: a process that has not received a response cannot immediately determine whether the remote process has crashed or whether the message is simply delayed. Asynchronous models are important because they accurately reflect internet and cloud environments.
      </p>
    </div>

    <KeyTerms
      terms={[
        { term: 'Asynchronous System', definition: 'A distributed system with no fixed upper bounds on computation speed, message delay, or clock sync.' },
        { term: 'Unbounded Delay', definition: 'The condition where message delivery or execution can take an arbitrarily long time.' },
        { term: 'Failure Ambiguity', definition: 'The inability to distinguish between a crashed node and a slow network connection.' },
      ]}
    />

    <SpeakerNotes>
      Emphasize the core insight: on the internet, you can never prove a quiet server is dead versus just slow.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 36: FAILURE MODEL
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="failure-model-foundations"
    eyebrow="03 · FUNDAMENTAL MODELS"
    title="Failure Model: Defining Component Malfunctions"
    layout="split-sidebar"
    unit="BIT452CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        Failures are an unavoidable reality in distributed systems. A failure model defines the ways in which components can fail and the behavior that can be expected from a faulty component.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        A process may stop completely because of a crash. A communication channel may lose a message. A process may respond too slowly. In severe cases, a faulty component may behave arbitrarily and provide conflicting data to different peers.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        By defining an explicit failure model, system designers determine which failures must be tolerated and what recovery mechanisms are required to maintain correct operation.
      </p>
    </div>

    <KeyTerms
      terms={[
        { term: 'Failure Model', definition: 'A formal specification of malfunction types and behaviors a distributed system is designed to handle.' },
        { term: 'Fault Envelope', definition: 'The defined boundaries of failure types and quantities a protocol can withstand.' },
        { term: 'Fault Tolerance', definition: 'The ability of a system to continue operating correctly according to specification despite failures.' },
      ]}
    />

    <SpeakerNotes>
      Introduce the concept of designing for failure as the core mindset of distributed systems engineering.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 37: CRASH FAILURE
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="crash-failures"
    eyebrow="03 · FUNDAMENTAL MODELS"
    title="Crash Failure: Permanent Process Halting"
    layout="split-sidebar"
    unit="BIT452CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        A crash failure occurs when a process stops executing and does not perform any further operations.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        For example, a server may lose power, experience an operating-system panic, or terminate due to a critical software exception.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        The difficulty is that other processes may not immediately know that the failed process has crashed. A lack of response could indicate either a crashed process or a delayed message, requiring timeout mechanisms and failure detectors.
      </p>
    </div>

    <KeyTerms
      terms={[
        { term: 'Crash Failure (Fail-Stop)', definition: 'A failure mode where a process halts execution completely and produces no further observable actions.' },
        { term: 'Crash-Recovery Model', definition: 'A variant where a crashed process can restart, read persistent disk logs, and rejoin the active cluster.' },
        { term: 'Failure Detector', definition: 'A software mechanism that uses heartbeats and timeouts to suspect or detect node crashes.' },
      ]}
    />

    <SpeakerNotes>
      Explain fail-stop vs crash-recovery. In real cloud systems, servers reboot after crashing, which is why persistent Write-Ahead Logs (WAL) are required.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 38: OMISSION FAILURE
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="omission-failures"
    eyebrow="03 · FUNDAMENTAL MODELS"
    title="Omission Failure: Missing Communication Actions"
    layout="card-grid"
    unit="BIT452CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-5 my-auto">
      <Card title="Send-Omission" badge="Sender Buffer" glow="indigo">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Process fails to transmit.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          A process executes a send command, but the message is never placed into the network buffer due to memory pressure or operating system errors.
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="Channel Omission" badge="Network Loss" glow="rose">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Dropped in physical transit.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          A message enters the network but is dropped before reaching the destination, caused by buffer overflows in routers or damaged physical links.
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="Receive-Omission" badge="Receiver Buffer" glow="cyan">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-1">Host fails to deliver message.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          A message arrives at the destination network interface, but the host operating system fails to deliver it to the application process.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Break down the three places a message can be lost: sender socket, intermediate network, or receiver buffer.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 39: TIMING FAILURE
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="timing-failures"
    eyebrow="03 · FUNDAMENTAL MODELS"
    title="Timing Failure: Violating System Deadlines"
    layout="split-sidebar"
    unit="BIT452CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        A timing failure occurs when an operation takes longer than the time allowed by the system's timing assumptions.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        For example, a server may eventually compute the correct response, but the response arrives after the deadline required by the application.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        Timing failures are particularly critical in real-time systems, such as industrial control and live multimedia streaming. In these environments, a response that arrives late is considered invalid, even if its data is accurate.
      </p>
    </div>

    <KeyTerms
      terms={[
        { term: 'Timing Failure', definition: 'A failure where an operation produces a correct response, but delivers it outside a required time deadline.' },
        { term: 'Real-Time System', definition: 'A system where correctness depends on both the logical result and the exact time it is delivered.' },
        { term: 'Clock Drift Failure', definition: 'A condition where a local clock drifts faster than the system maximum allowed rate.' },
      ]}
    />

    <SpeakerNotes>
      Contrast timing failures with omission failures: in timing failures, the data arrives, but it arrives too late to be useful.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 40: BYZANTINE FAILURE
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="byzantine-failures"
    eyebrow="03 · FUNDAMENTAL MODELS"
    title="Byzantine Failure: Arbitrary and Malicious Behavior"
    layout="split-sidebar"
    unit="BIT452CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        A Byzantine failure represents an arbitrary failure in which a component behaves incorrectly in ways not limited to simply stopping or losing messages.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        A Byzantine-faulty process may send incorrect information, deliver conflicting messages to different peers (equivocation), or behave inconsistently over time.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        Byzantine failures are challenging because other processes cannot assume that a responding node is behaving correctly. Tolerating these failures requires redundancy, agreement protocols, cryptographic authentication, and quorum majorities of at least 3f + 1 nodes to tolerate f faulty nodes.
      </p>
    </div>

    <KeyTerms
      terms={[
        { term: 'Byzantine Failure', definition: 'An arbitrary failure mode where a node can transmit incorrect, conflicting, or corrupted data.' },
        { term: 'Equivocation', definition: 'The act of a faulty node sending conflicting information to different peers in the same round.' },
        { term: '3f + 1 Quorum', definition: 'The minimum total number of nodes needed to reach consensus despite f arbitrary Byzantine faults.' },
      ]}
    />

    <SpeakerNotes>
      Introduce the classic Byzantine Generals Problem (Lamport). Explain why blockchain networks (Bitcoin/Ethereum) must use Byzantine fault tolerance.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

