  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 43: WHY ARCHITECTURE ALONE IS NOT ENOUGH
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="why-architecture-insufficient"
    eyebrow="04 · SYNTHESIS & ANALYSIS"
    title="Why Architecture Alone Is Not Enough"
    layout="split-sidebar"
    unit="BIT452CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        Knowing the architecture of a distributed system does not tell us everything about its behavior.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        For example, an architecture may contain three replicated servers, but this alone does not explain what happens if one server crashes.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        Similarly, knowing that two processes communicate does not tell us whether messages can be delayed indefinitely or whether there is a guaranteed maximum transmission time. To reason about these situations, architecture must be combined with interaction and failure assumptions.
      </p>
    </div>

    <KeyTerms
      terms={[
        { term: 'Structural Topology', definition: 'A static visual map showing component placement and physical or logical connection lines.' },
        { term: 'Dynamic Semantics', definition: 'The runtime execution rules that govern state changes, timing, and error responses.' },
        { term: 'Model Completeness', definition: 'Evaluating a system using architecture, interaction timing, and failure models together.' },
      ]}
    />

    <SpeakerNotes>
      Warn students against "architecture-only" thinking: a box on a diagram doesn't tell you how a system behaves when cables get cut.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- ========================================================================
       SLIDE 44: SYSTEM MODELS AND DISTRIBUTED ALGORITHMS
       ======================================================================== -->
  <Slide
    id="models-and-algorithms"
    eyebrow="04 · SYNTHESIS & ANALYSIS"
    title="System Models and Distributed Algorithms"
    layout="split-sidebar"
    unit="BIT452CO: Distributed & Cloud Computing"
  >
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        Distributed algorithms operate under assumptions about the environment in which they execute.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        For example, a leader-election algorithm must make assumptions about communication and failures. A distributed consensus algorithm must consider the possibility that processes may fail or messages may be delayed.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        If an algorithm assumes synchronous communication but executes where messages experience arbitrary delays, its correctness may not hold. System models define the conditions under which distributed algorithms operate correctly.
      </p>
    </div>

